problems.problem:
Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers, and let $P$ and $Q$ be two $m \times n$ arrays of $0$s and $1$s arranged in $m$ rows and $n$ columns. For example, for $m = 3$ and $n = 4$ one such array is $$\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.$$ Suppose the two arrays have the following four properties:

- in every row of $P$, the numbers never increase from left to right;

- in every column of $P$, the numbers never increase from top to bottom;

- the sum of the numbers in each row of $P$ equals the sum of the numbers in the same row of $Q$;

- the sum of the numbers in each column of $P$ equals the sum of the numbers in the same column of $Q$.

Prove that the number in row $i$, column $j$ of $P$ equals the number in row $i$, column $j$ of $Q$ for all $i = 1, \dots, m$ and $j = 1, \dots, n$.

On a large $100 \times 100$ chessboard, small boards of size $1 \times 3$ and $3 \times 1$ are to be placed so that:

(i) every square of the chessboard is covered by at most one small board;

(ii) the small boards together cover every square of the large board except one;

(iii) the sides of the small boards are parallel to the sides of the squares of the large board.

Suppose that this requires $H$ boards of size $1 \times 3$ and $V$ boards of size $3 \times 1$. Find all possible pairs $(H, V)$.

Determine whether there exists a positive integer $N$ satisfying all of the following:

(a) $N$ is divisible by $2^{2023}$ but not by $2^{2024}$;

(b) $N$ has only three distinct digits, none of which is zero;

(c) exactly $99.9\%$ of the digits of $N$ are odd.
